    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey can u explain how lactic acid actually works in your muscles during high intensity training? like why does it make them burn and how does the body get rid of it after the workout is over?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "During high-intensity exercise, your muscles produce lactic acid when oxygen runs low, as they rapidly convert glucose for energy. The burning sensation you feel isn't from the lactic acid itself, but from the release of hydrogen ions it generates, which acidify the muscle tissue and irritate nerve endings.\n\nAfter your workout, the body clears this by converting lactic acid back into pyruvate, which is then used for energy or turned into glucose by the liver (the Cori cycle). This clearance process happens naturally within minutes to a couple of hours as blood flow returns to normal and oxygen is restored.",
      "source": "target",
